# Click-Once directory
publish/

# Publish Web Output
*.[Pp]ublish.xml
*.azurePubxml
# Note: Comment the next line if you want to checkin your web deploy settings,
# but database connection strings (with potential passwords) will be unencrypted
*.pubxml
# DEF-429, 2026-09-17: our two publish profiles ARE checked in, deliberately. The stock warning
# above is about profiles that embed a connection string or a web-deploy <Password>. Ours embed
# neither - both are FileSystem publishes with no credential, verified by grep before tracking.
# What they DO carry is deploy safety policy that was previously on one machine only:
#   <Content Remove="wwwroot\uploads\**" /> and App_Data\uploads\** - written after a publish
#   wiped the production PDF store (2026-04-23, and a factor in 2026-06-15);
#   the dev-config and appsettings.*.template.json exclusions;
#   EnvironmentName=Production, without which the deployed app loads placeholder config.
# A fresh clone used to get none of that. If a profile ever gains a real credential, pull it back
# out of git rather than re-broadening this pattern.
!GCCWebApp/Properties/PublishProfiles/*.pubxml
*.pubxml.user
*.publishproj

# Microsoft Azure Web App publish settings. Comment the next line if you want to
# checkin your Azure Web App publish settings, but sensitive information contained
# in these scripts will be unencrypted
PublishScripts/

# NuGet Packages
*.nupkg
# NuGet Symbol Packages
*.snupkg
# The packages folder can be ignored because of Package Restore
**/[Pp]ackages/*
# except build/, which is used as an MSBuild target.
!**/[Pp]ackages/build/
# Uncomment if necessary however generally it will be regenerated when needed
#!**/[Pp]ackages/repositories.config
# NuGet v3's project.json files produces more ignorable files
*.nuget.props
*.nuget.targets

# Microsoft Azure Build Output
csx/
*.build.csdef

